From 5e565f0f2c1d4ebb56202a678476d0e42ef0443b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joeri Van Dooren <ure@on6ure.be>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:01:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] audio, ptt: the X6100/X6200 radio's own software as the sound
 card and PTT (OpenX6X00)

On the radios, OpenX6X00's GUI owns the audio and the keying: ardopcf and
Mercury reach them through its Winlink modem socket, and XARA now does
too. --audio x6x00 with --capture and --playback both the socket
(/run/ardop.sock) and --ptt x6x00, behind a cargo feature of the same
name.

audio/x6x00.rs: a thread connects, and again every second while the GUI
is not there, and says hello in the socket's protocol 2 (12000 Hz, keys
of 30 s at most, XARA's own watchdog). The receiver comes in 20 ms R
messages, up to 48 kHz with XARA's resampler; read() is paced by it and
returns zeros at the wall clock's pace while the radio transmits (no R
then) or the GUI is away. write() takes the audio down to 12 kHz and
sends it as T only between a P 1 and its P 0, which the PTT sends under
the same lock, so a transmission is exactly what the GUI was asked to
key; what the pump writes in the moment before the PTT thread keys is
held and sent right after the P 1. P 0 makes the GUI play out every T it
has, then unkey. The path is the capture (the GUI's 20 ms fragments and
the resamplers), the key (the GUI's checks and the base) and the GUI's
playback stream, as constants, 25, 50 and 80 ms until they are measured
on the radios; --x6x00-path-ms CAPTURE,KEYING,OUT sets them. latency()
gives all three, the round trip the modem allows for, and the new
capture_latency() the capture's, which goes to the scheduler's rx_lag:
the PTT is held after a transmission for the keying and the playback
only, as xara 0.1.3 held it. The capture waiting to be read is said in
0.1.3's words past 100 ms and at each doubling ("xara is falling behind
real time"), which this fork's pump does not say.

The receiver late: when no R comes for 60 ms while XARA listens, read()
gives silence in its place and the reads go on at the wall clock's pace.
When that audio comes after all, in a rush (the GUI or the system
stalled), its time has been counted already: as much of it as there was
silence is dropped, the oldest first, and for 100 ms the reads keep the
wall clock rather than the rush's pace. XARA's clock stays the air's, and
it says "audio: no receiver audio from the GUI for N ms (XARA heard
silence in its place); M ms of it came late, all at once, and was
dropped". Kept, the rush ran XARA's clock ahead of the air by the stall,
and waiting to be read it was said as XARA falling behind real time,
which the radio's supervisor takes for the processor short for XARA: on
an X6100, 107 to 817 ms of capture waiting with 1 ms of processing a
block. XARA's own key is not counted (no R comes then by design, nor in
the GUI's turn back to the receiver), nor a GUI that is away.

daemon.rs checks the options together, as for the (tr)uSDX (one socket
for both, --ptt x6x00 with --audio x6x00 and the other way round) and
takes the PTT from the backend, through the new Audio::ptt(). With
--audio x6x00 the station starts with listening off: it answers no call
until its host sends LISTEN ON, which Pat does only to listen. XARA runs
from the radio's start and has no host until Pat calls out, so an answer
would key the radio with nobody there. With unit tests on a fake GUI
(the hello, R paced and resampled, zeros while keyed and without the
GUI, T only between P 1 and P 0, the held first block, a GUI that goes
and comes back, one that closes at once tried once a second, the stop's
P 0, the latency, the backlog said, late audio after silence dropped and
not said as falling behind, a GUI back on time losing nothing, XARA's own
key not counted) and daemon.rs's own (a call to a
station made for the radio is not answered, the same station with
another backend answers it).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
